Classes — the unit of code.

Java is an object-oriented language, and the class is the unit that everything is built from. A class is a blueprint. It says what a certain kind of thing knows — its data — and what it can do — its behaviour. And from that one blueprint, you stamp out objects: individual instances, each with its own copy of the data.

The whole idea of a class is to keep related data, and the behaviour that operates on it, together in one place. Think about a bank account. It knows its balance, and it can deposit into that balance. The number, and the operations that make sense on that number, live side by side. In the class, balance is a field — that's the state. Deposit is a method — that's the behaviour. Bundling them is the core move of object-oriented design. You don't leave loose data floating in one place and the functions to manipulate it somewhere else.

Now, the difference between a class and an object is the difference between a blueprint and a house. The class is the type; an object is a value of that type, created with the new keyword. So if you make one bank account and then another, you've created two different, independent accounts. Deposit a hundred into the first, and its balance is a hundred, while the second account's balance is still zero. One class, many objects, and each one has its own separate balance. They share the same shape — both are bank accounts — but they carry separate state. It's the difference between the cookie cutter and the actual cookies.

Inside a method, the fields refer to this particular object's data. And when a name is ambiguous — say a parameter happens to have the same name as a field — you write this, meaning "the object the method was called on". So in a set-balance method, this dot balance is the field, while plain balance is the parameter. You'll see this constantly; it's simply how an object refers to itself.

So why model with classes at all? Because classes let you name the concepts in your problem — order, customer, invoice — and give each one a small, self-contained set of responsibilities. Code organized this way reads like the domain it models. It stays local, so you change how an account works in exactly one place. And it composes, because bigger objects are built out of smaller ones. The rest of this module builds directly on this: hiding a class's internals, constructing objects properly, relating classes through inheritance and interfaces, and choosing how to combine them.
